    "problem_statement": "# Unix socket files not being unlinked when closing sockets\n\n**Describe the bug**\nWhen using Unix sockets with Gunicorn, the socket files are not being properly unlinked when the server shuts down. This leaves stale socket files in the filesystem.\n\n**To Reproduce**\nSteps to reproduce the behavior:\n1. Create a simple WSGI application\n2. Run Gunicorn with a Unix socket binding\n3. Stop Gunicorn\n4. Notice that the socket file still exists in the filesystem\n\n```python\n# Example reproduction script\nimport os\nimport socket\nimport time\n\n# Create a Unix socket\nsock_path = '/tmp/test_gunicorn.sock'\nif os.path.exists(sock_path):\n    os.unlink(sock_path)\n\n# Create a socket and bind it\ns = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\ns.bind(sock_path)\n\n# Import the close_sockets function\nfrom gunicorn.sock import close_sockets\n\n# Close the socket using the function\nclose_sockets([s], unlink=True)\n\n# Check if the socket file still exists\nif os.path.exists(sock_path):\n    print(f\"ERROR: Socket file {sock_path} still exists!\")\n    print(f\"Expected: Socket file should be removed\")\nelse:\n    print(\"SUCCESS: Socket file was properly unlinked\")\n```\n\n**Expected behavior**\nWhen Gunicorn shuts down, it should unlink (remove) the Unix socket files it created.\n\n**Environment info**\n- OS: Linux\n- Python version: 3.10\n- Gunicorn version: latest\n\n**Additional context**\nThis issue can cause problems when trying to restart Gunicorn, as it may fail to bind to the socket if the file still exists from a previous run."
